title: The King's statement
new-page: true

## The next day arrives, time-skip into you being in library already
## The King had announced his speech, defending Darlyne and denouncing the rumours
## The Imperial Forum is on an emergency session
## The nobility is currently on a heated debate between support and opposition of the speech
## The King is reportedly on his way to attend the emergency session
## There are whispers of a compromise solution by putting Darlyne on an independent trial led by Supreme Court
## You decide to go on a sneak visit to the Forum 

## Finished, but could be polished further

- @new_day: The next day has arrived.

@new_day
subtitle: And so have another set of tasks for the day.
new-page: true

After everything that has happened yesterday, the day seems to pass by in a blink, and before you realize it, you find yourself already working on another paper in the library this noon.

You are not alone this time, however, as there just so happens to be a young lady sitting next to you, casually reading through what looks to be an artbook.

Her name is Rea. She is known for her expertise as an artist, and the Royal Family had even commissioned multiple works of art from her previously.

She maintains a good relationship with many people inside the palace, including Princess Darlyne herself, and you have also acquainted yourself with her quite a few times before.

"By the way, Levon, have you seen the King's speech earlier this morning?" Rea says as she shifts her gaze from the artbook towards you. "Fiercely defensive, apparently. Even tried to denounce those newspapers for spreading rumours before."

- @what: "...The King's speech?"

@what

"...The King's speech?" 

You blink a few times as you repeat what she just mentioned.

"Yeah, it was all over the newspapers too, you know?" Rea says with a very casual tone. As she pauses for another few seconds, she fully turns her sitting position towards you. "...So I assume you haven't heard of it?"

Slightly embarrassed, you admit that you haven't heard much of it. Though you could recall that there were probably numerous chatters and gossips related to the speech, you didn't really understand what they were talking about at all.

"Well, I suppose that makes sense." Rea lets out a sigh as she closed the artbook. For some reason, however, you could see a small smile forming on her lips. "There's also the whole thing with the Imperial Forum too, so... do you want the full details?"

You nod once more, now eagerly prepared to listen.

Rea takes a deep breath. "Alright, here we go..."

- @kings_speech: On to the King's Speech.

@kings_speech

**The King's Speech**

The King's speech from earlier this morning was said to revolve around the recent rumours swirling around Princess Darlyne.

The King started by addressing that the Royal Palace *is* indeed aware of recent reports regarding the Princess's alleged activity. However, His Majesty immediately followed it with statements defending his daughter's innocence. Furthermore, he even threatened that *"... newspapers that continue to report such baseless rumours ... will be hammered for defamation by the rule of law"*.

It was a relatively short speech, lasting no more than ten or so minutes. Unsurprisingly, the reaction to it has been quite varied among the populace. Many agreed with the King's speech — though not a few agreed simply because it's a statement from the King — but many others also took problems with how dismissive the King was to what they view as a reported and legitimate concern.

What has certainly been *way* more interesting, however, is the reaction of the nobility, which perfectly segues into the next part, which is...

- @imperial_forum_response: The Imperial Forum's responses.

@imperial_forum_response

**The Imperial Forum's response**

Not long after the King's speech ended, several nobles immediately rushed into the Imperial Forum to call for an emergency session this noon. The agenda, of course, is to discuss the appropriate response — or perhaps more accurately, reaction — to that speech.

The Imperial Forum, simply put, is split between three camps: those publicly opposing the speech, those supportive of the speech, and those preferring to defer making their decisions for the time being. The debate had been a heated one so far. It is *still* ongoing, in fact.

The opposing nobles know that they wouldn't stand a chance if they were standing alone against *the literal King himself* in any potential future lawsuits, so of course they would much rather deal with this early while it's still hot off the press. They *have* to do something against this — some of the victims from the allegations are under the protection of these nobles, after all.

To add fuel to the fire, however, the King was also reportedly on his way to attend the emergency session. His Majesty is probably already inside, watching through the chaos as we are talking in here right now.

- @what_now: So... what happens next?

@what_now

...God knows what will happen next.

However, there have been some rumours flying out of the Forum that there is about to be a compromise solution.

An unprecedented one, in fact.

The compromise solution is to put Princess Darlyne Nightbloom on trial.

- @wait_what

@wait_what
title: ...What?
new-page: true
go-to: option_visit

"...What?"

It's the only word that could come out of your mouth after hearing such a suggestion. Her Royal Highness, *The Princess herself*, on trial?

You could see Rea holding back a laugh on her face. In its place, instead, is a rather sarcastic smile. You can feel that the smile isn't exactly directed towards you, however.

"Well, what else would you expect for them to suggest?" Rea shrugs nonchalantly as she picks another artbook from the table. "The factions were almost evenly split as well, so that would be a guaranteed headache either way."

- @silent_again: ...

@silent_again

Once again, you don't really know what to say in response. Perhaps it's because there are too many informations that you need to process in such a short time.

Rea is already focusing once more on the artbook she is reading, leaving you with your own papers and thoughts once again.

You begin to ponder what to do after you're done with this paper.

@option_visit

